abstract: >-
  A real Littlewood polynomial has all coefficients +1 or -1. For every
  epsilon in (0,1) and every integer N >= exp(exp(exp(10^12 log^2(2/epsilon)))),
  there is a real Littlewood polynomial of length N whose modulus lies between
  (1-epsilon)sqrt(N) and (1+epsilon)sqrt(N) on the whole unit circle.
  In particular, for every N >= exp(exp(exp(10^12 log^2 4))) the error at
  length N can be taken to be 2 exp(-10^-6 sqrt(log log log N)). This makes explicit the ultraflat
  construction of OpenAI's preprint "Ultraflat real Littlewood polynomials"
  (October 5, 2026), which gave no rate. The new arguments are a hypergraph
  matching lemma with constants explicit in the edge size, replacing the
  Pippenger-Spencer theorem, and an exponential decay estimate for the
  auxiliary polynomials. The bound is not practical. The proofs were written
  and checked with AI assistance and have not been reviewed by a human expert.
keywords:
  - Littlewood polynomials
  - ultraflat polynomials
  - flat polynomials
  - hypergraph matching
  - discrepancy
